Every element $f \in R$ is of the form $ue$ where $u$ is a unit and $e$ is an idempotent. Then Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/04MG" data-tag="04MG">10.26.5</a> shows $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is Hausdorff. On the other hand, $\mathbf{N}$ with the discrete topology can be viewed as a dense open subset. Given a set map $\mathbf{N} \to X$ to a Hausdorff, quasi-compact topological space $X$, we obtain a ring map $\mathcal{C}^0(X; k) \to R$ where $\mathcal{C}^0(X; k)$ is the $k$-algebra of locally constant maps $X \to k$. This gives $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathcal{C}^0(X; k)) = X$ proving the universal property.